read-string-does-not-skip-comments — pure --dialect bricscad specificity: (read "; comment\nobj") -> NIL on real BricsCAD, unlike AutoCAD/clautolisp
======================================================================================================================================================

Status: OPEN, root cause fully characterized (2026-09-08) — confirmed
a BricsCAD-only reader quirk, not a general AutoLISP-family behavior;
narrowed to a concrete --bricscad-dialect fix. Found while building an
unrelated project (autocad-gui-prototype, loader.lsp).

Summary
-------

  (read ";; foo\nbar")

  BricsCAD V26 (real, macOS)    -> NIL
  BricsCAD V25 (real, Windows)  -> NIL   (same behaviour, cross-platform)
  AutoCAD 2022 (real, Windows)  -> BAR   (skips the comment, like load)
  clautolisp (any dialect)     -> BAR

Confirmed pure `--dialect bricscad` specificity, not vendor-family-wide:
AutoCAD's reader matches clautolisp's own (lenient) behaviour; only
BricsCAD (both platforms tested) returns NIL the instant it encounters
a comment before finding a real object — leading whitespace/newlines
before the comment, or before the object, don't matter, only whether a
`;`-comment is the first non-blank thing the reader meets. This also
resolves "To check" items 2 and 3 below (kept, answered inline) — no
further vendor/platform probing needed before fixing.

`load` (reading a form from a FILE) skips `;`-comments before handing
a form to the reader, on both sides -- that much is consistent. But
`read` applied directly to a STRING containing leading comment lines
does NOT skip them on real BricsCAD: it returns NIL (as if EOF were
reached immediately), while clautolisp's own `read` strips comments
the same way `load` does, silently matching a naive assumption about
AutoLISP source-file handling rather than (apparently) real AutoLISP
reader semantics on a bare string.

Concretely (autocad-gui-prototype/loader.lsp): a small loader read a
metadata file (a foo.alpm, itself starting with four `;;;;` header-
comment lines, then a real `(alpm-define-system ...)` form) via
read-line + strcat (reconstructing the whole file into one string) and
a single `(read whole-string)` on the result. Under clautolisp this
returned the expected form every time headless -- 130/130 tests green,
including an end-to-end reload of loader.lsp. On real BricsCAD V26 it
silently returned NIL, every single time, for all three files
affected (each starting with the same four-line comment header) --
plain `load`-based file loads elsewhere in the exact same project were
completely unaffected (they never call `read` on a string, only
`load` on a file). Confirmed via a live BricsCAD probe: the
reconstructed string was verified byte-correct (exact length, exact
content, comments included) via `strlen`/`princ`, then `(read that-
string)` still came back NIL. Root cause pinned down only after
several other hypotheses (SECURELOAD/TRUSTEDPATHS, boundp reliability
on `C:` symbols, literal-path identity, `foreach` vs. plain function
nesting, file encoding) were each tested live on BricsCAD and ruled
out first — this is the confirmed, reproducible bottom of it, not a
guess.

To check
--------

  1. The autolisp-spec pages for READ — does the spec text say
     anything about comment-skipping being a LOAD/file-reader
     responsibility rather than READ's own? Given (2)/(3) below are
     now answered, the spec page should end up documenting this
     explicitly as a --bricscad divergence (AutoCAD/clautolisp skip
     the comment and return the following object; BricsCAD returns
     NIL), not left silent or generic.
  2. ANSWERED: real AutoCAD (2022, Windows) skips the comment and
     returns the following object, matching clautolisp — NOT matching
     BricsCAD. Real BricsCAD (V25 Windows, V26 macOS — cross-platform,
     same product) consistently returns NIL. This is a genuine
     BricsCAD-specific reader quirk, not a general AutoLISP-family
     read semantics question.
  3. ANSWERED (for the leading-comment case that matters here):
     BricsCAD returns NIL as soon as a `;`-comment is the first
     non-blank thing the reader meets, before any real object —
     leading whitespace/newlines before the comment or the object are
     tolerated either way. Comments trailing AFTER a first object (or
     between multiple objects in one string, for a caller doing
     repeated `read`s) are NOT characterized here — narrower, separate
     question if it ever matters to a caller.

Fix
---

Pure `--dialect bricscad` specificity (confirmed, not a guess) — the
fix is narrow and dialect-scoped, not a change to clautolisp's own
default reader behaviour:

  - Under `--bricscad` specifically: `read` (string form) should
    return NIL when the first non-blank content it encounters is a
    `;`-comment, before any real object — matching real BricsCAD V25/
    V26 exactly. Under `--autocad` and `--clautolisp`, KEEP the
    current comment-skipping behaviour as-is — it already matches
    real AutoCAD 2022, and clautolisp's own dialect has no vendor to
    match at all, so leniency there is a deliberate, fine convenience,
    not a bug.
  - This is exactly the kind of per-dialect behavioural divergence
    deferred-clautolisp-out-of-dialect-warnings.issue's mechanism is
    for — but that issue is about a MISSING-operator warning (READ
    exists in every dialect here, so it wouldn't fire). If the fix
    above lands as a real per-dialect behaviour change, a warning is
    arguably not even needed anymore for this specific case (--bricscad
    would just behave correctly) — but a caller relying on --bricscad's
    leniency today (there may be other code out there doing what
    autocad-gui-prototype's loader.lsp did) would silently start
    getting NIL where it used to get an object once this fix lands.
    Worth a one-time NEWS/changelog callout when shipped, even without
    the general warning mechanism.

Workaround shipped downstream (autocad-gui-prototype, not clautolisp
itself): the caller now strips comment-only lines itself before ever
calling `read`, rather than relying on `read` to do it — see
loader.lsp `fs-loader--comment-line-p` / `fs-loader--slurp`,
commit bfc057d.

Found by
--------

Original macOS/BricsCAD V26 repro: autocad-gui-prototype/loader.lsp
live-BricsCAD debugging session, 2026-09-08 (Claude Sonnet 5, with pjb
driving BricsCAD interactively). Cross-platform/cross-vendor
confirmation (BricsCAD V25 Windows matches V26 macOS; AutoCAD 2022
Windows matches clautolisp instead) supplied directly by pjb the same
day, from separate own testing/knowledge — not re-derived via a live
probe in this session.

Related: deferred-clautolisp-out-of-dialect-warnings.issue (the
general per-dialect-behavior warning mechanism, discussed above as an
alternative/fallback to the direct --bricscad fix).
